A student writes this JavaScript code to validate a registration form before submission:
```javascript
const form = document.getElementById('reg-form');
form.addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  const password = document.getElementById('pass').value;
  if (password.length < 8) {
    alert('Password must be at least 8 characters!');
  }
});
```
When tested with a 4-character password, the alert box pops up, but as soon as the user clicks 'OK', the webpage immediately refreshes and submits the form to the server anyway! What critical line of code is missing?

sub_questions.answer:
```javascript
const form = document.getElementById('signup-form');
const passwordInput = document.getElementById('password');
const confirmInput = document.getElementById('confirm-password');
const errorMsg = document.getElementById('error-msg');

form.addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  const pass = passwordInput.value;
  const confirmPass = confirmInput.value;

  // Check if passwords match and meet minimum length
  if (pass.length < 8) {
    event.preventDefault(); // Stop form submission and page refresh
    errorMsg.textContent = 'Password must be at least 8 characters long!';
    errorMsg.classList.remove('hidden');
    passwordInput.focus();
    return;
  }

  if (pass !== confirmPass) {
    event.preventDefault(); // Stop form submission and page refresh
    errorMsg.textContent = 'Passwords do not match. Please verify!';
    errorMsg.classList.remove('hidden');
    confirmInput.focus();
    return;
  }

  // If validation passes, clear errors and let form proceed
  errorMsg.textContent = '';
  errorMsg.classList.add('hidden');
});
```

Explanation of Logic:
1. `event.preventDefault()`: Intercepts the submit event, halting the browser's native submission before client-side checks finish.
2. Strict Comparison: Uses `pass !== confirmPass` to prevent type coercion false-positives.
3. Accessible DOM Feedback: Populates `errorMsg.textContent` and toggles CSS class via `classList`, providing immediate visual feedback without reloading the page.

```javascript
const catalog = document.getElementById('course-catalog');
const registeredList = document.getElementById('registered-list');
const totalCreditsDisplay = document.getElementById('total-credits');
let totalCredits = 0;

// Event Delegation: Attach a single listener to the parent catalog
catalog.addEventListener('click', function(event) {
  // Check if the clicked element is an 'Add Course' button
  if (event.target.classList.contains('add-btn')) {
    const button = event.target;
    const courseName = button.dataset.course; // Read data-course attribute
    const credits = parseInt(button.dataset.credits, 10); // Read data-credits

    // 1. Create a new list item node dynamically in memory
    const li = document.createElement('li');
    li.textContent = `${courseName} (${credits} Units)`;

    // 2. Append the new node to the registered list
    registeredList.appendChild(li);

    // 3. Update the cumulative credit units counter
    totalCredits += credits;
    totalCreditsDisplay.textContent = totalCredits;

    // 4. Disable button to prevent duplicate course registration
    button.disabled = true;
    button.textContent = 'Added';
  }
});
```

Advantages of this Architecture:
1. Memory Efficiency: Replaces 40+ individual event handler closures with a single lightweight listener on the parent container.
2. Dynamic Compatibility: Any courses dynamically added via AJAX or user interactions are handled automatically without re-binding listeners.
